Subject: [PATCH 12/12] Record what governs this repository, and adjudicate the
 Manager contradiction

Five repositories carry a constitution and they are not the same constitution.
v2 sits in Claude, Joe-Assistant and Publisher; v3 in Claude-3 and Library; this
repository -- the one that actually runs -- carries neither. All of them name a
Manager component, and CLAUDE.md section 5.6 forbids one while
tests/test_repository_doctrine.py fails a build over it. An agent cold-starting
in one of those three repositories reads its constitution and builds toward a
component this repository rejects.

Manager is neither created nor removed. docs/MANAGER.md is the only document in
any repository that states its implementation status -- named in planning, never
built, authorises no code -- and the constitutions describe an intended
organisational shape rather than ruling on what the program may contain. Two
different questions, and nothing recorded which one each document answered.

GOVERNANCE.md says, in one place, what governs here and why: this file declares
itself the entry point, its clauses are the only ones in the programme enforced
by a test that fails a build, and this is the only repository containing the
running program. Authority determined from what the repository obeys, not from
which document is newest.

Nothing is deleted and nothing is merged. Section 7 forbids editing an old
decision to hide its history, so every superseded document stays byte for byte
where it is; a pointer beside it is what stops it being read as current.

DISPATCH_CONSTITUTION_v3's own status is left open and recorded as such. Its
header reads "Current Controlled Constitution - v3 Replacement Draft", no
repository holds an approval record, and inferring ratification is the
manufactured approval section 4 forbids. It is registered ADVISORY -- true under
either reading, and no code decision rests on it.
